    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d939">

                <pb facs="0822" n="813" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg813"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.48/771</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Secretary of
                        State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-10-07">October 7, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The British Ambassador,<anchor n="29" xml:id="d939-f1"/> accompanied by Mr. N. M.
                    Butler, Counselor of the British Embassy, called at his request. The Ambassador
                    spoke about our proposal to supply some foodstuffs to Spain under given
                    conditions. He said that his Government was in harmony with this view and that
                    this was the psychological time for such action. He seemed to have in mind olive
                    oil as one commodity that might be considered as part payment for any loan we
                    might make for foodstuffs, adding that this would be in addition to cork and
                    quicksilver.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ordell</hi>] H[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >ull</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d939-f1" n="29" xml:id="d939-f1-content">
                <p>Lord Lothian.</p>
            </note>
